## std/emit — algebraic effect for append-to-sink LLVM IR output.
##
## Operation Piezoelectric Effects, Phase 5, Milestone 5.3 (compiler
## dogfooding pilot).
##
## Declares the `Emit` effect with three ops matching the existing
## codegen_util API: `emit` (raw append), `emit_line` (append + newline),
## `emit_int` (format i64 in place). Callers inside a
## `with Emit { emit: h, emit_line: h2, emit_int: h3 } do ... end` block
## write to an ambient sink instead of threading an `out: Int`
## StringBuilder descriptor through every codegen helper.
##
## Phase 5.3 uses the same bridge shape as 5.1 (Diag) and 5.2
## (State<TcCtx>): `codegen_ctx::cg_ctx_set(out)` records the active
## sink; the default handlers forward to `codegen_util::cg_emit` etc.
## Leaf codegen helpers body-wrap themselves with
## `with Emit { … } do -> emit("…") … end`, exercising the effect
## machinery while keeping the outer signature stable during the
## incremental migration.
##
## All three ops are `Void` so handlers compose cleanly in expression
## positions (match arms, if-branches) without coercing sibling
## expressions to `Int`. The handler's `(op_name, argc, argv): Int` ABI
## is unchanged — the runtime simply discards the handler's return when
## the op declares Void.
##
## Emit is NOT in the runtime's blessed default set: calling `emit(...)`
## outside a `with Emit { ... }` block fails typecheck with QZ0610.
## Intentional — silently dropping codegen output would produce a silent
## broken binary, far worse than a compile error.

effect Emit
  def emit(s: String): Void
  def emit_line(s: String): Void
  def emit_int(n: Int): Void
end
